// =================
// Content Interface
// =================

type Content interface {
	GetNewDocuments(context.Context, *GetNewDocumentsRequest) (*GetNewDocumentsResponse, error)

	// GetSigningKeys returns the public keys the transparency log's
	// signatures are made with, so that a consumer reading the log with
	// GetNewDocumentsRequest.verification can verify it without leaving
	// the API it is already talking to.
	//
	// The same keys are served as a JWKS at the unauthenticated
	// GET /signing-keys and mirrored into the archive bucket. Those remain
	// the way an independent verifier with no credentials, checking
	// archived content, gets them. This is the convenience for a consumer
	// that already holds a client.
	//
	// An independent copy is worth keeping either way: keys fetched from
	// the service that signed the log only prove it is internally
	// consistent.
	GetSigningKeys(context.Context, *GetSigningKeysRequest) (*GetSigningKeysResponse, error)

	// GetDocument returns a single document, transparently falling back to
	// the archive if the requested version (or the whole document) has been
	// evicted from the hot data set. The document is returned in-band.
	GetDocument(context.Context, *GetDocumentRequest) (*GetDocumentResponse, error)

	// CreateImportUpload starts a bulk import upload: the client declares
	// the files that make up the import set and gets a presigned PUT URL
	// back for each of them. The files are uploaded directly to object
	// storage; the service only brokers the URLs. Files are gzipped NDJSON
	// with one import envelope per line, and they are loaded in the order
	// they were declared, so declare them in chronological (quarterly)
	// order.
	//
	// To resume an upload that was cut short, call CreateImportUpload
	// again with the upload ID and the same file declaration: fresh URLs
	// are returned for every file, and only the ones that are missing or
	// were interrupted need to be PUT again. Re-uploading a completed file
	// is harmless - finalization verifies sizes and hashes, not upload
	// counts. An upload that is never finalized is eventually cleaned up.
	//
	// Requires dist_admin, unlike the service's dist_read baseline, and
	// fails with FailedPrecondition when the deployment has no archive
	// bucket configured - there is nowhere to upload to.
	CreateImportUpload(context.Context, *CreateImportUploadRequest) (*CreateImportUploadResponse, error)

	// FinalizeImportUpload declares an upload complete and turns it into
	// an import job. The service verifies that every declared file is
	// present in the bucket with the declared size and sha256 - a missing
	// or mismatching file is a FailedPrecondition, and the upload stays
	// open so the file can be re-uploaded and finalization retried -
	// records the import, and starts loading asynchronously. The returned
	// import ID is what GetImportStatus takes; the upload ID is consumed
	// and invalid afterwards.
	//
	// Loaded documents go through the same store path as live content:
	// document versions are upserted and the events enter the transparency
	// log hashed and signed like any others, carrying the import's source
	// so that consumers can tell bulk-imported content from the live
	// repository flow. The load is resumable and idempotent per document
	// version - a service restart continues where it left off.
	//
	// Requires dist_admin and an archive bucket, as CreateImportUpload.
	FinalizeImportUpload(context.Context, *FinalizeImportUploadRequest) (*FinalizeImportUploadResponse, error)

	// GetImportStatus returns the recorded state of an import job: where
	// the load has got to, and what went wrong if it failed.
	//
	// Requires dist_admin.
	GetImportStatus(context.Context, *GetImportStatusRequest) (*GetImportStatusResponse, error)

	// ListImports lists import jobs, most recent first.
	//
	// Requires dist_admin.
	ListImports(context.Context, *ListImportsRequest) (*ListImportsResponse, error)

	// CancelImport cancels an import. Cancellation is terminal: the job
	// stops being claimable by the loader, the imports queued behind it
	// proceed, and its hold on index maintenance is released. An
	// un-finalized upload can be cancelled too, which retires its upload
	// ID.
	//
	// Only a job that is not actively loading can be cancelled: a load
	// that keeps failing backs off between passes with status "failed",
	// which is when a cancel lands. Documents the job already published
	// are signed log entries and stay published - remediation of loaded
	// content is per-document erasure, not cancellation.
	//
	// Requires dist_admin.
	CancelImport(context.Context, *CancelImportRequest) (*CancelImportResponse, error)

	// ListPublishedVersions lists the publication events of an interval,
	// newest first: what was published, in the order it happened. Every
	// stored version of every document of the requested types whose
	// publication time falls in the interval, superseded ones included and
	// marked as such, so that a correction sits at the minute it was made
	// rather than under the document's latest version.
	//
	// This is the same log GetNewDocuments serves, read by publication time
	// instead of by event ID. It is a range scan over the stored versions,
	// not a search: the inputs are document types, an interval and facet
	// values, the order is chronological, and there is no relevance,
	// free-text or cross-type ranking to be had.
	//
	// Any interval can be listed, however old: retention drops document
	// bodies and keeps the bookkeeping the listing is made of. Past
	// inline_horizon the entries come back deferred and their documents are
	// streamed in with GetDocumentVersions.
	//
	// Intended for backward-anchored types - articles, flashes, editorial
	// information - where the question is what was published when. See
	// ListPlannedVersions for the calendar view of forward-anchored types.
	ListPublishedVersions(context.Context, *ListPublishedVersionsRequest) (*ListPublishedVersionsResponse, error)

	// ListPlannedVersions lists what is planned for an interval, earliest
	// first: the current version of every document of the requested types
	// whose resolved anchor date falls in it.
	//
	// Only current versions, deliberately. A planning item's revisions are
	// working state rather than publications, and a calendar showing five
	// revisions of one item is noise - so unlike ListPublishedVersions this
	// never carries version history. An item rescheduled out of the
	// interval leaves it entirely rather than staying behind under its old
	// date.
	//
	// Intended for forward-anchored types: planning items and calendar
	// entries.
	ListPlannedVersions(context.Context, *ListPlannedVersionsRequest) (*ListPlannedVersionsResponse, error)

	// GetDocumentVersions loads a batch of document versions. It is the
	// batch form of GetDocument, and it is how a client streams in the
	// documents a listing left deferred.
	//
	// Responses that carry documents bound how many they carry, because a
	// document whose body has aged out of the hot store is an archive read
	// each and loading a whole page of them would make the response time a
	// function of how far back the caller browsed. Entries beyond that
	// bound come back with deferred set and no document; pass their UUID
	// and version here to fetch them, in whatever order and at whatever
	// rate suits the client - typically what is on screen first, and the
	// rest as the reader scrolls.
	//
	// The same bound applies here: ask for more than the server will load
	// in one response and the surplus comes back deferred again, so a
	// client that keeps requesting what it did not get makes progress
	// rather than looping.
	GetDocumentVersions(context.Context, *GetDocumentVersionsRequest) (*GetDocumentVersionsResponse, error)
}

